Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||05:52 AM||||I'm planning a custom dash and hoped to use some digital gauges from Dakota Digital (or similar).  I was under the assumption that I could simply figure out which wires went to what, detach them from the stock plug, and reattach them to the individual gauges.  I was cybersurfing earlier and noticed (on a Ferrari kit site) that someone offers custom made wiring harness that plugs in to the stock harness somewhere.  The site said something about needing to take 80 hours to figure out the wiring if I don't use thier harness (this was dealing with conventional gauges, not digital if it matters).  What gives?  Can I just cut wires (assuming I know what they go to) and reattach to the new gauges or what?  I don't want to spend $650 on gauges just to find that I'll need to spend another $300 on another harness.  Here's the link to the harness site.  <A HREF="http://www.carsecrets.com/interior.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carsecrets.com/interior.html</A> <P>Thanx for the help.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||09-03-2000||07:32 AM||||Ah - I remember now I promised you pin-out data of some sorts. What was that again?<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000002||hugh||09-03-2000||08:29 AM||||I have Cyberdyne gauges in my custom 70 Chevelle.I assume all the manufacturers use the same wiring setup.All the gauges need are 12 volt supply,ground,and to be connected to their correct sending unit.The easiest thing to do may be to run whatever wire is needed between the gauges and the engine compartment to their sending units and disregard the original wiring.The gauge manufacturer may have a recommended wire size,if they don't you can probably use as small as  you can buy.Like 18 gauge.It should be easy to route the wires under the car in such a way that they are protected from damage.Just be careful that all your connections are clean and tight.Many people just put the wires on the gauges by using the same nut that holds the gauge in the panel,WRONG!Tighten the gauge down with one nut per stud,then use a second nut to tighten the wire to the gauge.Hope this is understandable and helps.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||08:36 AM||||I forgot all about that.  No problem on that (it was a digidash from a Buick).  This is a totally different project (I'll actually finish one of these projects <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).  I am designing a custom dash.  Fairly straight surfaces (no compound curves--above my ability), high grade vinyl and smoked Lexan faces (going for some stealth).  I'm planning on purchasing some digital gauges (separate units but speedo/tach will be in the same can) from Dakota Digital.  These will fit behind the Lexan.  Hopefully, when the car is off, they will be invisible.  I hoped they would be easy to wire up as long as I could match the factory wiring with the wiring on the gauges (same/same??).  However (isn't there always one of those? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), as I was cybersurfing yesterday, I came upon a site for some Ferrari/Fiero/VDO gauges.  That's the link above.  Says something about needing a separate wiring harness.  The harness info is at the bottom of the linked page.<P>I just need to know if I can match wire for wire with the new/digital gauges (already asked about the compatibility with the VSS--DD said that it shouldn't be a problem) or if I need some kind of custom harness made.  If I only need some inexpensive (?) tech advice, no problem.  On the other hand, if I need a few hundred dollars worth of wiring harness, then it might get cost prohibitive very quickly.  The gauges alone will be about $650US.  Add to that the cost of the aftermarket switches (still can't find anything for p/w), and other materials and I'm going to have a fairly expensive dash.<P>Sorry for the bandwidth.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||08:42 AM||||hugh--I'm mostly concerned with the speedo/tach and compatibility with things such as CC, VSS, etc.  You probably don't have an ECM in your Chevelle (love to see some pics!).  The way I understand it, everything has to run through the ECM.  I would assume most of the gauges wouldn't be a problem (volts, H2O, oil, fuel), but I don't want to spend another few hundred bucks just trying to make the speedo compatible.||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||09-03-2000||08:57 AM||||I can understand if the speedometer is necessary for computer operation,but if it isn't the digital speedometer is driven directly off the original location and the tach reads from the ignition.As for cc and vss,I have no idea what they are.I will post pictures in the mall section,as the car is for sale so I can use my garage for my GT.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||09:00 AM||||CC=cruise control and VSS=Vehicle Speed Sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-03-2000||10:54 AM||||1. You can't remove all the old dash. You need parts of it so the VSS feed will get divided by 2 and sent to the ECM or the car won't run right.<P>2. Don't cut anything. Use those wire tap connectors. The ones with the little metal tab you squash into the wires and then you close a little door over the tab. Then if this doesn't work you still have the OE harness intact.<P>3. you likely can't hook the new speedo directly to the VSS. You'll need to use the same 2000ppm feed that the ECM gets.<P>Sorry, I don't have an easy way to trace the dash harness. Most of it is in the various diagrams in the shop book or ALLdata but even that may not be everything.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||01:57 PM||||Ogre--That's the info I didn't want to hear, but needed.  Where could I go to find out exactly what needs to be done?  I know nothing about this stuff.  Is there something I could use inline to fix the speedo/VSS problem?  (If it matters, I've driven the car around without the dash/gauges and I didn't notice a difference.)<P>This is getting frustrating.  If theogre and Cliff can't figure it out, maybe I'm doomed.  Maybe I'll just put the original dash back in the car and forget about it.  Good thing I didn't order the gauges yet.  There is something to be said for the older carbbed cars.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||02:22 PM||||OK.  I just sat down, had a nice cold Diet A&W Root Beer and a cigarette, and calmed down some.  (I don't do frustration well.)  I know that there's some way to get this figured out.  I guess, even if I have to take some college classes, I'll make this happen.  I didn't want to spend lots of extra money on the dash, but I'm not giving up.  Who can figure this out for me?  What info do you need and at what price?  This is the only stumbling block as far as I can tell (other than some carpentry abilities which I hope to learn as I go).||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-03-2000||03:07 PM||||No idea exactly where the VSS driver/divider is. I don't have all the diagrams for it and they don't likely say where the fool thing is.<P>GM doesn't make the dash schematics available even to dealers as far as I can tell. Just the normal drawings like are in haynes or ALLdata that spread the thing out over 15 pages and even then the drawings don't tell you allot of stuff.<P>If you drove it and the divider was unplugged then you should have a VSS code in the ECM. That would maybe narrow things a bit.<P>I can also tell you from Cliff's notes and some reports from others that if there is an aftermarket tach filter you could have problems. Some of them don't work right with digital gauges. The stock one has to be in pretty good shape or it may not work either. To confuse it even more some aftermarket tachs may hate the filter alltogether. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||03:32 PM||||I just happened to have a spare dash in my computer room (don't ask! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) so I decided to take it apart.  I thought that the speedo was plugged in the same as the tach.  Nope.  It's a totally separate unit.  Therefore I can limit my search to 9 wires/connection points.  (Still doesn't help alot, but it's progress.)  I also just emailed a guy I found on the Fiero Online Guide (something like that--fiero@dagobah.something?).  His site shows a digital speedo/tach that he designed and built.  Hopefully he'll either be able to give me the right answers or build me whatever it is that I need for the speedo.  As far as the tach...I'm hoping that the current tach filter is still in good shape.  If not, I'll just have to search for another OEM one.  If it comes right down to it, I can live without a tach while I'm trying to figure out how to make it work.  I appreciate the info, thoughhhh.<P>Do you foresee any problems with the other misc gauges (volts, H2O temp, oil press, fuel)?  According to Dakota Digital, the fuel gauge will come with the correct sending unit (GM--0 to either 30 or 90 ohms).  I would assume that the others have no bearing on the ECM (or vice versa) other than to shoot various codes?  The gauges have a built in warning.  They're supposed to flash whenever the reading gets out of line (user programmable).  I'm hoping for a plug-n-play on those.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||09-04-2000||03:08 PM||||The ECM doesn't know or care about anything with the dash but the VSS feed. It has its own temp sensor etc. It gets it's RPM feed directly from the ignition module if it even uses one. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||rockcrawl||09-04-2000||03:49 PM||||OK, here's what I know about the subject. There is a buffer built into the circitry of the fiero dash. it takes the signal from the VSS and sends it to the speedo, the CC, and to the ECM. If you remove the factory dash, the ECM will not get the VSS signal it's looking for, and the CC won't work. But all hope is not lost, many other GM cars from the same era use a separate buffer. It is usually a green, white, or yellow plastic box about the size of a cigarette pack, with a label that says "four out buffer". It receives the VSS signal and gives four output signals, some are divided or multiplied (different PPM) depending on what they are for. One of these little gadgets connected to the fiero harness would solve tha problem. I'll get you the part # and wiring diagram later. <P>The tach should be a simple hookup with the exception of the possible filter problem.<P>As for the other guages, there should not be any problems. My Cyberdyne guages came with their own senders. The temp sender also feeds the ECM, so the original one should be retained. If your guages are supposed to be compatible with GM senders, that makes it real easy. I would recommend using the existing wiring. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||10
Z||000014||theogre||09-04-2000||03:57 PM||||the gauge sensor operates the guage and the warning light. (2 green wires on it.)<P>The ECM has it's own temperature sensor. (a black and a yellow wire on it.)<P>The 2 circuits are physically and electrically independant.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cliff Pennock||09-04-2000||05:00 PM||||I'm a bit tired (had a bad week - don't ask) so I haven't read the above posts thoroughly. But let me give you some pointers.<P>First of all, you can completely empty out everything in the dash <B>except</B> the speedometer print. The most important part of this print is that it take the 4000p/mile input, cleans it up, and outputs a 2000p/mile to the ECM. Like Ogre said, if you've driven around without a dash, the ECM would eventually set the VSS errorcode.<P>You could get rid of the speedometer-print if you have the knowledge to build your own filter/divider. This is not a lot of work but why re-invent the wheel?<P>Everything else in the dash is of no importance for the drivability of the car. Nothing else is output to the ECM but those 2000p/m.<P>I have no idea why that site says you need a special wiring harness since this is total BS. If you give me all specs of those gauges, I can probably tell you exactly how to connect the wires.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||09-04-2000||05:12 PM||||I just received an email (right in the middle of reading this thread as a matter of fact) from the guy who built his own digital speedo/tach.  He seems to think he could build me a matchbox sized device/board that, I guess, would go inline between the gauge and the wiring.  Basically the same principle as what you guys are talking about.  Only problem is that he'll be on vacation until the 3rd of October.  I guess this will give me time to complete the roughed out stage of the dash.  Hopefully I'll have everything hooked up and ready to go by then.  I'll just need to install his device and make the finishing touches.  I'd more than appreciate your help, Cliff, but I know how busy you are.  If this guy doesn't come through, I'll be sure to send you the info on the speedo and see if you can figure something out for me.  I'm glad to hear that the rest of the gauges are a much easier prospect.  I was beginning to get worried.  (Of course I then realized that this is <B>THE</B> Fiero Forum.  If you guys can't fix it, nobody can! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  You guys are the best, hands down!||||reg||1
